The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
case NFT_ITER_UPDATE:
    m = pipapo_maybe_clone(set);
    if (!m) {
        iter->err = -ENOMEM;
        return;
    }
    nft_pipapo_do_walk(ctx, set, m, iter);
    break;
```
Reason:
During `NFT_ITER_UPDATE` iteration (e.g., netlink release/deactivation), the `pipapo_maybe_clone()` function unconditionally allocates a clone of the set using `GFP_KERNEL`. Under memory pressure, this allocation can fail, causing `iter->err = -ENOMEM` and a subsequent WARN splat in `nft_map_deactivate` (line 845 of nf_tables_api.c). The clone is unnecessary for many update operations (like deactivation) and should only be performed for the flush command.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
case NFT_ITER_UPDATE_CLONE:
    m = pipapo_maybe_clone(set);
    if (!m) {
        iter->err = -ENOMEM;
        return;
    }
    nft_pipapo_do_walk(ctx, set, m, iter);
    break;
case NFT_ITER_UPDATE:
    if (priv->clone)
        m = priv->clone;
    else
        m = rcu_dereference_protected(priv->match,
                                      nft_pipapo_transaction_mutex_held(set));
    nft_pipapo_do_walk(ctx, set, m, iter);
    break;
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a new iteration type `NFT_ITER_UPDATE_CLONE`, used only by the flush set command. Cloning via `pipapo_maybe_clone()` is now limited to this case, avoiding unnecessary memory allocations during other update operations. For `NFT_ITER_UPDATE`, the code uses an existing clone (`priv->clone`) if available, otherwise falls back to the original set representation (`priv->match`). This prevents the GFP_KERNEL allocation failure that caused the WARN splat.

